Iterative Method:
Definition:
The iterative factorial method calculates the factorial of a number by repeatedly multiplying the current result by each integer from 1 to n using a loop.
Time Complexity: O(n)
Conclusion:
The iterative method is simple and memory-efficient because it does not create recursive function calls. 
  It is generally preferred when memory usage is important.

Recursive Factorial:

The recursive factorial method calculates the factorial by repeatedly calling the same function with a
smaller value until it reaches the base case, where the factorial of 0 or 1 is 1.
Time Complexity: O(n)
Conclusion:
The recursive method provides a simple and mathematically intuitive implementation of factorial. However, it requires additional stack memory for every 
recursive call, making the iterative method more memory efficient.
